+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes reach connected browsers over WebSockets served by the project's own connection service, so update delivery carries no realtime vendor dependency or recurring vendor fees. The project operates connection capacity and reconnect handling itself.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed together around this choice. The change store keeps replayable event revisions and publishes each committed revision reliably to the connection service. Delivery manages socket sessions and fans committed revisions out to connected browsers. A browser whose socket drops shows a disconnected state rather than an unchanged board, reconnects, and catches up from the last revision it applied.
 
