 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for committed-change delivery.** The project runs its own connection service, so fast updates carry no realtime vendor fees and no provider coupling. Accepted costs: the project operates connection capacity and reconnect handling, and the change store must provide replayable revisions and reliable publication. Alternatives considered: five-second HTTPS polling was rejected because every browser repeats reads and every update waits up to five seconds, which makes cursor queries the hot path; a hosted realtime service was rejected because it brings recurring fees and provider-specific subscription and publication coupling, in exchange for less connection operations work.
